WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:10.630
In our previous video, we have taken a look
at the process of Pollination.

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.380
Can
you recall the process?

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.070
This process occurs in flowering plants.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.560
the pollen
grains are transferred from the anther to

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.670
the stigma of the same or a different
flower.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:27.039
We have also taken a look at the different
types of pollination.

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:31.199
Self-
Pollination and Cross Pollination.

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:35.230
Now let us see what exactly happens after
pollination.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:39.700
The next and most
important step is that of Fertilisation!

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.080
Now, what is this process exactly?

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:49.090
Fertilisation is simply the process of fusion
of male and female gametes to give rise to

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.350
a new embryo.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:55.170
Before understanding the process, let us understand
these terms which are

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.190
new for us!

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:00.989
Firstly let us get to know what gametes are!

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:05.680
Gametes are the specialised cells
which serve the function of reproduction.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.800
we find these cells in organisms that

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.840
carry out sexual reproduction.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:17.440
For now we only need to know that gametes
will be different in the males and females.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.180
And sexual reproduction is where
the gametes from both male and female individual

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.710
will unite to give a zygote.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.340
What is zygote?

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.600
It is the single cell structure formed from
the fusion of male

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.290
and female gametes.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:37.189
This single cell further develops into an
embryo.

00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:39.229
what is an embryo by the
way?

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:43.650
It is the structure that will further develop
into an organism.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.969
So gametes
are the special cells, called sex cells present

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:51.969
in organisms that participate in
sexual reproduction.

00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:54.479
Fusion of the two gametes, one from a male
and the

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:59.149
other from a female will give rise to a zygote
which will further develop into an

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:01.189
embryo.

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:03.939
This embryo grows and develops into the baby
organism.

00:02:03.939 --> 00:02:04.939
In sexually reproducing organisms:
Male and female gametes  Sex cells

00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:05.939
Fusion of gametes  Formation of zygote
Zygote  Embryo  Organism

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:06.939
(Note: Write develops above the arrows in
the last line, that zygote  Embryo

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:11.760
 Organism)
These were a few terms that we had to know.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.769
Now let us get back to the fertilisation process
in plants.

00:02:14.769 --> 00:02:17.620
Let us define it again.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.370
It is the process of fusion of male and female
gametes to give rise to a new

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.230
embryo.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:28.760
after pollination is successfully accomplished
how does fertilisation occur in

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:29.760
plants?

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.180
Let us zoom into this structure of a typical
Pistil, the female reproductive part

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:36.590
of the flower.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.370
The Pollination process has caused the pollen
grains to safely

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.910
land on the stigma which is seen here!

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:48.450
For fertilisation to occur, the male gametes
need to reach this lower part of the

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:52.690
pistil, called ovary, where the female gamete
awaits it!

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:57.409
The female gamete or
the egg cell is present inside the ovary.

00:02:57.409 --> 00:03:03.239
To form a zygote the female gamete
has to fuse with the male gamete!

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:05.300
Now how will this happen?

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.390
It is made possible by a structure called
the pollen tube!

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.769
This tube is formed
from the pollen grain itself.

00:03:11.769 --> 00:03:18.209
The tube travels down the complete style and
ultimately reaches the ovary.

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:20.720
The tube carries along the male gamete that
is

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.680
safely taken to the ovary.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.690
Now once the gamete reaches the ovary, the
two-fuse giving rise to the

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.349
formation of a single cell.

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:33.180
This is the zygote and the zygote will further
develop

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.720
into an embryo.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:40.510
Zygote formation in plants marks the completion
of Fertilisation.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:42.400
what happens next?

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:49.189
What will happen once fertilisation is successfully
accomplished? after fertilization the ovary

00:03:49.189 --> 00:03:54.019
develops into a fruit and ovules
develop into seeds.

00:03:54.019 --> 00:03:55.530
What is an ovule?

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:59.709
An ovule is a part of the female
reproductive system that will develop into

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:02.549
seeds after fertilisation.

00:04:02.549 --> 00:04:04.769
This is all
we need to know for now.

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:07.519
We will get into the structure and functions
of ovule

00:04:07.519 --> 00:04:09.659
in higher grades.

00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:13.710
So getting back, the ovule forms the seed
which is nothing but a structure

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.590
containing the embryo inside it.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.360
the baby plant!

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:23.900
A baby plant that is yet to
develop means it needs utmost protection.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.790
That is why we have the seed!

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:30.830
A seed remains safe and guarded by a seed
coat which protects the inner

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:35.700
delicate parts and the embryo during unfavourable
conditions.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.720
So how does a plant grow from this single
seed? when seeds are sown in the

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.560
soil, and when they achieve favourable conditions.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.970
they germinate to give us a
new plantlet.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:52.720
Now the next question is - how do seeds get
rooted every time in the soil?

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.760
Let
us get to know the concept of seed dispersal

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:01.030
and a few more interesting
concepts in the next video!

